# sumela-removed-files.list — CORE paths a release retires (v0.20.0+).
#
# scripts/update.sh (and its PowerShell twin) reads this file from the NEW release's tree, so a
# release lists its own removals here, in the same commit that deletes the files.
#
# One row per retired path, three TAB-separated fields:
#   <version>	<path>	<successor|->
#     <version>    the release that removes <path> (X.Y.Z)
#     <path>       the CORE path, relative to the install root
#     <successor>  the CORE path that now carries its content, or "-" when none does
#
# An upgrade from the installed version I to the new version N acts on every row with
# I < <version> <= N whose <path> the new release no longer ships:
#   your copy is upstream's copy of I (unmodified)   remove: the file is deleted
#   modified, successor "-"                          keep-removed: kept, recorded as removed-upstream
#   modified, a successor named                      carry: kept; your hunks against I go to
#                                                    .sumela/.upgrade/<path>.carry, to move into the
#                                                    successor; you then keep or drop the orphan
# Without a valid base for I nothing counts as unmodified, so nothing is removed.
#
# '#' comments and blank lines are ignored; a CR before a line end is ignored. A row whose path or
# successor is not a CORE path (or holds a "." or ".." segment) is ignored, with a warning. Never
# edit or delete a released row: an install upgrading across that release later still needs it.
